==English==
{{wikipedia}}
[[file:Closed human eye, superior view.jpg|right|thumb|Eyelid (with {{l|en|eyelashes}} growing from it)]]

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ḱley-|id=cover}}
From {{inh|en|enm|eyelidd}}, {{m|enm|eye-led}}, {{m|enm|eiȝelid}}, {{m|enm|eghe-lydd}}, {{m|enm|yȝe-lydd}}, {{m|enm|ehlid}}, {{m|enm|yhelidd}}, equivalent to {{af|en|eye|lid}}. Cognate with {{cog|stq|Oogenlid|t=eyelid}}, {{cog|fy|eachlid|t=eyelid}}, {{cog|nl|ooglid|t=eyelid}}, {{cog|nds-de|Ooglidd|t=eyelid}}, {{cog|de|Augenlid|t=eyelid}}.

Generally superseded non-native {{noncog|enm|palpebre|t=eyelid}}, borrowed from {{noncog|la|palpebra|t=eyelid}} (see Modern English {{m|en|palpebra}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈaɪ.lɪd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-eyelid.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪlɪd|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q48228}}A thin skin [[membrane]] that covers and moves over an [[eye]].
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Job|41|18|page=645|column=2|passage=By his neeſings a light doth ſhine, and his eyes are like the '''eye-liddes''' of the morning.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000|author=Joshua P. Warren|title=The Evil in Asheville|page=246
|passage={{...}} the frightened or insane eyes of an animal, sometimes with '''eyelids''' closed in escapeful slumber.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2009-01-14|author=Natasha Singer|title=Love the Eyelashes. Who Is Your Doctor?|journal=w:The New York Times|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2009/01/14/health/research/14lash.html
|passage=Allergan, the company that turned an obscure muscle paralyzer for '''eyelid''' spasms, Botox, into a blockbuster wrinkle smoother, hopes to perform cosmetic alchemy yet again.}}

